messages:
maria (devex): Quick question about recommended retry behavior for streaming responses + idempotency. We saw a customer hit duplicate partial outputs when a connection drops and the client retries. They also saw 429 vs 5xx behavior differ across regions. Any clear guidance we should document in the SDKs?

ben (sdk): TL;DR: require an idempotency key for non-streaming requests. Streaming is trickier — we should expose a resume token in chunk metadata and recommend clients use it to resume instead of re-sending the whole request.

maria (devex): Curious about backoff params: stable recommendation (base, cap, jitter) and how to behave on 429 vs 5xx? 429 sometimes gives Retry-After header, sometimes not. :thinking:

anya (runtime): For 429: honor Retry-After when present. If missing, treat like rate limiting: full-jitter exponential backoff with lower max (eg. base=200ms, cap=10s). For 5xx (transient infra), backoff with longer cap (eg. base=200ms, cap=30s) and fewer total attempts by default.

sam (eng-sre): Also add circuit-breaker at SDK level to avoid thundering herd on blacklisted endpoints. If many clients retry aggressively, we can overload the routing tier. Recommend client-side cap on concurrency per host.

linda (prod-eng): For streaming duplicates: server should emit a monotonic chunk sequence id or a stream_resume_token in the final/each-chunk metadata. If the client reconnects and provides the resume token, server can skip already-sent tokens. If resume token missing, treat resumed request as new and require idempotency semantics (see below).

ben (sdk): Suggested minimal SDK pattern (pseudo):
```
// JS pseudo
async function sendWithResume(req) {
  const idempotencyKey = req.idempotencyKey || uuid();
  let resumeToken = req.resumeToken; // optional
  for (let attempt=0; attempt < MAX_ATTEMPTS; attempt++) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': idempotencyKey }, body: buildBody(req, resumeToken) });
      if (res.status === 429) throw new RateLimitError(res.headers.get('Retry-After'));
      return streamParser(res.body); // yields only new tokens if resumeToken used
    } catch (err) {
      const backoff = jitteredBackoff(attempt, base=200, cap=(err instanceof RateLimitError?10000:30000));
      await sleep(backoff);
      // on reconnect, if server returned resume token before disconnect, use it
      resumeToken = getLatestResumeToken();
      continue;
    }
  }
  throw new Error('exhausted retries');
}
```

anya (runtime): Important: if you can't resume, do NOT automatically retry streaming by resending the same idempotency key if your request included side-effects (uploads, DB writes). For pure generate-only requests it's OK but for anything with side-effects require a server-side dedupe token that the backend understands.

maria (devex): What about token duplication in the client when partial chunks were delivered and the client replays the whole response? We want to avoid double-appending to user timelines. Any client-side dedupe pattern?

ben (sdk): Simple client dedupe: keep last N tokens' hashes and drop duplicates within a short window. Better: rely on server resume token + sequence numbers. If server returns chunkSeq, client should append only chunks with chunkSeq > lastSeenSeq.

sam (eng-sre): And for metrics/alerts: surface both 429 rate and retry volume in telemetry. If retry rate spikes, trigger oncall — likely an upstream capacity/regression.

linda (prod-eng): One more gotcha: some intermediate proxies will transform Retry-After units or swallow it. Recommend server also return a short-lived header eg X-Rate-Limit-Backoff to give clients a safe default.

maria (devex): Nice. Can we summarize into 3 bullets for the SDK docs?

ben (sdk): Sure: 1) For non-stream requests: require Idempotency-Key; server dedupes. 2) For streaming: prefer resume tokens + chunkSeq; if resume token unavailable, fall back to non-resume with conservative retries and client-side dedupe. 3) Backoff: full-jitter exponential, honor Retry-After when present, shorter caps for 429 vs 5xx, and circuit-breaker on sustained failures.

anya (runtime): I'll add server-side support for stream_resume_token and chunkSeq in the next runtime patch.

maria (devex): I'll draft the SDK doc blurb and add code snippets for Python/JS. Anyone want to review?

ben (sdk): Happy to. Low-effort review ping when ready. :thumbsup:

retry-bot: Note: policy auto-apply for rate limits updated. See /policies/rate-limits v2.

maria (devex): Thanks all — closing the thread once the doc PR lands.
